  the event for SOME singleton Q = {x} (a run of round T contains an input never asked before):
    X = [50], L = 1, n = 10: probability 0.9146;  L/n = 0.1000;  each fixed x: 0.018293
    X = [200], L = 1, n = 20: probability 0.9539;  L/n = 0.0500;  each fixed x: 0.004769
    X = [1000], L = 1, n = 50: probability 0.9759;  L/n = 0.0200;  each fixed x: 0.000976
  so Theorem 3.2(a) is a statement for each fixed Q, not for all Q at once.
