== G: Proposition 2.5: beta(A)/L <= rho', and rho' for the exact imitator A = P
  200 random pairs: smallest (mismatch probability - TV) = 1.397e-12  (>= 0 required)
  the exact imitator A = P, a human who gives the answer f(x) with probability 1 - e and one
  other answer with probability e: mismatch probability per asked call 2 e (1 - e), although
  beta(A) = 0.  Certificate (a) (no mismatch in m rounds) is passed with probability (1-2e(1-e))^m:
    e = 0.000: rho' = 0.0000;  Pr[no mismatch in m = 300 rounds] = 1.000e+00;  certified beta/L by (b) at m -> infinity: 0.0000
    e = 0.001: rho' = 0.0020;  Pr[no mismatch in m = 300 rounds] = 5.488e-01;  certified beta/L by (b) at m -> infinity: 0.0020
    e = 0.010: rho' = 0.0198;  Pr[no mismatch in m = 300 rounds] = 2.480e-03;  certified beta/L by (b) at m -> infinity: 0.0198
    e = 0.100: rho' = 0.1800;  Pr[no mismatch in m = 300 rounds] = 1.394e-26;  certified beta/L by (b) at m -> infinity: 0.1800
  with theta = 0.1 (faults tolerated per call), R3 needs beta/(theta L) < 1, i.e. rho' < 0.1:
  for e = 0.1 the exact imitator has rho' = 0.18 and certifies nothing, for every m.
  answers of h bits of entropy (free text): mismatch probability >= 1 - 2^(-h_min); for
    min-entropy  1 bits: rho' >= 0.5000
    min-entropy  3 bits: rho' >= 0.8750
    min-entropy 10 bits: rho' >= 0.9990
